window.docContents[145].resolve({"/The-standard-library/Headers/#Core-C______--The-standard-library--Headers":{"contents":"The header <vector> declares template <typename T> class vector; in namespace std. A vector is an object, created with new and reached by pointer. Its value  holds one location per element.The element type has values and a default, so it is not a class, a type of the library, a function type, void or . A vector therefore holds pointers to objects and never objects.The predicate  states this condition with Vector.isElement.Indexing gives a location, so v[i] may stand on the left of an assignment and bind a reference.A negative size and an index outside  have no derivation, and the evaluator gives error for them. C++17 defines a[n] as *(a.begin() + n) with no bounds check, which only at performs (N4659 §26.2.3, Table 88).\n\nThe header <functional> declares template <typename F> class function; in namespace std. The template argument is a function type . A std::function<F> is an object, since its module gives new a signature. It is created with new, reached by pointer and destroyed with delete. Its value  holds one location, its target, a closure, or  for the function with no target.The predicate  states this condition.The argument of new std::function<F>(λ) is a lambda, acceptable at its function type  by the rule T-Lambda of . The form new std::function<F>() is the default construction of C++ (N4659 §23.14.13.2.1 ¶1), which gives the function with no target.The call of the object, (*f)(e₁, …, eₖ) for a pointer f, is the rule CallFn of , a rule of the language, which reads the target and applies it. The function with no target has no derivation there, and the evaluator gives error, as the bad_function_call of C++ ends a program that does not catch it.\n\nThe header <cassert> declares void assert(bool condition);. In C++ assert is a macro, and with NDEBUG undefined a failed assertion calls abort (N1570 §7.2.1.1). Core C++ gives error, which ends with the same exit status.A failed assertion, assert(false), has no derivation.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009The standard library","header":"8.2. Headers","id":"/The-standard-library/Headers/#Core-C______--The-standard-library--Headers"}});